        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Both are safe, both need an electrician, and both physically prevent your generator energising the utility lines. Anyone telling you an interlock is the unsafe budget option is wrong — the interlocking plate provides the same guarantee a transfer switch does.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The genuine trade is flexibility against foolproofness. An interlock costs less and gives you every circuit in the panel, but correct operation depends on someone understanding the household load. A manual transfer switch costs more and covers a fixed list, but it is clearly labelled, hard to misuse, and the loads that would stall a generator are simply not on it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            So the deciding question is usually about people rather than hardware: who is likely to be home when the power goes out, and will they know what they are doing? Where that person is confident with the panel, fit the interlock and save the money. Where it might be anyone, the labelled switch earns its extra cost the first time it is used.
          </p>
        </div>


